Stating the type beside the pattern
A pattern may state the type it must match. In a local binding, write the type after the pattern:
main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = {
    let (low, high) : (Int, Int) = (20, 22)
    high
}
Ash checks the pattern against the stated type — here (low, high) against (Int, Int) — and the initializer against it as well, so a disagreement is a type error reported before the program runs. Running the program reports 22 : Int.

main : Unit -&gt; (Int, Bool)
main () = {
    let identity = fn(value) {
        value
    }
    (identity 42, identity true)
}


$ ash 01-local-identity.ash
(42, true) : (Int, Bool)

Optional connection: preserving callback effects
You can continue directly to the next Learn chapter. Return to this example after the effects lesson on callback rows, where requests, handlers, and effect rows have been introduced.
effect Bonus {
    amount : Unit -&gt; Int
}

request_bonus : Unit -&gt; Int ! {Bonus}
request_bonus ignored = perform Bonus.amount ()

main : Unit -&gt; (Int, Int)
main () = {
    let apply = fn((function, value)) {
        function value
    }
    let constant = fn(ignored) {
        40
    }
    let first = apply (constant, ())
    let second = handle (apply (request_bonus, ())) with Bonus {
        amount ignored =&gt; resume 2
    }
    (first, second)
}
The direct anonymous initializer lets apply generalize its callback’s input, result, and effect row. Identify the callback requirement at each call, then predict both integers. The second call places a handler around the callback’s request:
$ ash 07-local-effect-row.ash
(40, 2) : (Int, Int)
The pure callback returns 40; the effectful callback receives 2 from its handler. The row choice follows each callback, just as the input type follows each argument. Generalization preserves the row’s constraints and retains its effects. It introduces no interface requirements; those come from the contracts and evidence taught in the library path.

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = {
    let base = 40
    let add_base = fn(value) {
        base + value
    }
    add_base 2
}

$ ash 01-sequential-bindings.ash
42 : Int

Put mutually dependent functions in one group
A recursive group can contain several members. Write and on a new line for each member after the first:
type Count = Zero
    | Next(Count)

main : Unit -&gt; (Bool, Bool)
main () = {
    let rec even : Count -&gt; Bool = fn(count) {
        match count {
            Zero =&gt; true
            Next(rest) =&gt; odd rest
        }
    }
    and odd : Count -&gt; Bool = fn(count) {
        match count {
            Zero =&gt; false
            Next(rest) =&gt; even rest
        }
    }
    let two = Next(Next(Zero))
    (even two, odd two)
}
Count represents a nonnegative count by repeated Next constructors. Zero is even and not odd. Removing one Next reverses the question: evenness of the whole asks for oddness of the rest, and oddness asks for evenness. Definitions that call each other in this way are mutually recursive.
Both names enter scope before either initializer is checked. Both initializers are anonymous functions, so their calls to the other member wait until the functions are used. The let two binding begins the ordinary block remainder. Predict both results by alternating the calls until they reach Zero:
$ ash 03-mutual-functions.ash
(true, false) : (Bool, Bool)
Trace the first result as even two, then odd (Next Zero), then even Zero. The second trace ends at odd Zero instead. Each call still follows one smaller finite count.
Two sequential let bindings do not provide this mutual scope:
type Count = Zero
    | Next(Count)

main : Unit -&gt; (Bool, Bool)
main () = {
    let even : Count -&gt; Bool = fn(count) {
        match count {
            Zero =&gt; true
            Next(rest) =&gt; odd rest
        }
    }
    let odd : Count -&gt; Bool = fn(count) {
        match count {
            Zero =&gt; false
            Next(rest) =&gt; even rest
        }
    }
    let two = Next(Next(Zero))
    (even two, odd two)
}
At the initializer for even, which names have already entered scope? Does delaying its call change which names its body can use?
$ ash --message-format human --locale en 04-sequential-forward.ash
No term named `odd` is in scope.
  --&gt; /path/to/ash-doc/content/learn/defining-local-recursion/src/04-sequential-forward.ash:9:27
9 |             Next(rest) =&gt; odd rest
  |                           ^ This name could not be resolved here.
reason: resolve.unknown-name
The label marks odd inside the earlier function. That later binding is not in scope there, even though execution would call even after both bindings. Name scope is established from the source, not from a prediction about when the function will run. Restore the let rec and and group from the preceding program when the definitions depend on one another.

Build a cycle without traversing it forever
Recursive groups can also tie together nominal data. This is a different use of recursion from following a finite list. 05-guarded-cycle.ash gives each node a score and a next node, with no ending constructor:
type Trail = Step(Int, Trail)

main : Unit -&gt; (Int, Int)
main () = {
    let rec first : Trail = Step(20, second)
    and second : Trail = Step(22, first)
    match first {
        Step(left, rest) =&gt; match rest {
            Step(right, ignored) =&gt; (left, right)
        }
    }
}
The group reserves the two data locations, builds Step(20, second) and Step(22, first), and connects their recursive references. Each initializer starts with a fully applied constructor of its declared nominal type. The references are stored as fields rather than used to calculate their contents during initialization.
The result follows exactly two Step patterns. Predict which stored node supplies each returned integer, and whether that bounded observation needs an ending constructor:
$ ash 05-guarded-cycle.ash
(20, 22) : (Int, Int)
Following the next link after those two nodes would return to the first node. The cycle is finite stored structure, but an unrestricted walk has no ending case. The finite-list traversals in Learn and the Cases rely on reaching a base constructor; that assumption does not hold here.
Constructor guarding is not general lazy evaluation: Ash does not delay every subexpression until its value is needed. Initializers still evaluate ordinary nonrecursive subexpressions. The special allowance is for retaining recursive references through constructor or tuple construction, or delaying them inside an anonymous function body. It does not make arbitrary unfinished values available to computation.

interface Twice&lt;A&gt; {
    double : A -&gt; (A, A)
}

impl Twice&lt;Int&gt; {
    double value = (value, value)
}

replicate : A -&gt; (A, A) where Twice&lt;A&gt;
replicate item = double item

main : Unit -&gt; (Int, Int)
main () = replicate 21


$ ash 01-twice.ash
(21, 21) : (Int, Int)

A value a handler supplies
Sometimes a calculation needs a value it should not choose for itself. An effect declares the request; a surrounding handler answers it. 02-scale-price.ash requests the scale factor for a price:
effect Scale {
    by : Int -&gt; Int
}

price : Int -&gt; Int ! {Scale}
price base = perform Scale.by base

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = handle (price 20) with Scale {
    by factor =&gt; resume (factor * 3)
}
The declaration effect Scale names one operation, by : Int -&gt; Int. Inside price, perform Scale.by base makes the request, and the signature’s ! {Scale} records the outstanding need as part of the calculation’s contract. The effect rules and row notation describe both parts. The handler’s clause binds the requested integer as factor and answers with resume (factor * 3), which supplies the value and continues the waiting calculation.
Follow the request and work out the multiplication before running, again from this lesson’s src directory:
$ ash 02-scale-price.ash
60 : Int
price never chooses a factor; the handler does. The effects path builds its opening chapter, requesting a value, around this exchange.

type Option&lt;A&gt; =
    None
    | Some(A)

keep&lt;F : Type -&gt; Type, A&gt; : F&lt;A&gt; -&gt; F&lt;A&gt;
keep value = value

main : Unit -&gt; Option&lt;Int&gt;
main () = keep(Some(42))


F : Type -&gt; Type


ash 01-explicit-kind.ash

Some(42) : Option&lt;Int&gt;

pub interface Functor&lt;F : Type -&gt; Type&gt; {
    map&lt;A, B&gt; : (A -&gt; B, F&lt;A&gt;) -&gt; F&lt;B&gt;
}

to_bool : Int -&gt; Bool
to_bool value = value == 42

to_int : Bool -&gt; Int
to_int true = 42
to_int false = 0

pub map_pair&lt;F : Type -&gt; Type&gt; : (F&lt;Int&gt;, F&lt;Bool&gt;) -&gt; (F&lt;Bool&gt;, F&lt;Int&gt;) where Functor&lt;F&gt;
map_pair (numbers, flags) = (map((to_bool, numbers)), map((to_int, flags)))



pub import api (Functor, map)

pub type Box&lt;A&gt; = Box(A)

pub impl Functor&lt;Box&gt; {
    map (function, Box(value)) = Box(function(value))
}


import api (map_pair)
import carrier (Box, impl Functor)

main : Unit -&gt; (Box&lt;Bool&gt;, Box&lt;Int&gt;)
main () = map_pair((Box(42), Box(true)))

ash 01-map-pair.ash

(Box(true), Box(42)) : (Box&lt;Bool&gt;, Box&lt;Int&gt;)

pub interface Measure&lt;Key, Result&gt; where Key -&gt; Result {
    measure : Key -&gt; Int
}

pub impl Measure&lt;Bool, Unit&gt; {
    measure value = 42
}

pub interface Equal&lt;A&gt; {
    equal : (A, A) -&gt; Bool
}

pub impl Equal&lt;Int&gt; {
    equal (left, right) = left == right
}

pub same : (A, A) -&gt; Bool where Equal&lt;A&gt;
same pair = equal pair

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = {
    newtype Points = Points(Int)
    let points = Points(40)
    match points {
        Points(value) =&gt; value + 2
    }
}

$ ash 01-local-value.ash
42 : Int

Supply the exact method needed here
A local nominal can identify an exact interface application. In 03-exact-method.ash, Read&lt;A, B&gt; uses A to determine its result type B:
interface Read&lt;A, B&gt; where A -&gt; B {
    read : A -&gt; B
}

forward : A -&gt; B where Read&lt;A, B&gt;
forward value = read value

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = {
    newtype Token = Token(Int)
    let token = Token(40)
    let offset = 2
    with Read&lt;Token, Int&gt; {
        read (Token(value)) = value + offset
    }
    forward token
}
with Read&lt;Token, Int&gt; supplies one complete application, not a generic implementation head. Its read method unwraps a Token and adds the preceding offset value. The source becomes available only after every supplied method has checked; forward token is in that following remainder.
Identify the complete requirement at forward token and the preceding declaration that supplies it. Which ordinary value does the supplied method capture? Follow its calculation to predict the result before running it:
$ ash 03-exact-method.ash
42 : Int
The method unwraps 40 and adds the captured offset, which is two. At the call, the token fixes the selection key. The exact Read&lt;Token, Int&gt; source determines the result as Int and supplies the required method. Inside forward, its ordinary declared requirement carries that method to read. The global helper uses the supplied behavior without naming the local type.
This retains the earlier library model: a generic helper declares its requirement, and the caller supplies evidence. with adds a lexical source for one exact relation. It does not infer a new requirement or initiate local implementation search.
The application must be explicit and have no unresolved inference choices. Its key must contain an active local nominal. Here Token is the determinant; for an interface without an edge, all arguments form the key. A local nominal under another type constructor also counts, but one appearing only in a dependent result does not.

A module nominal does not establish a local key
Compare the earlier local declaration with a module-level Token:
interface Read&lt;A, B&gt; where A -&gt; B {
    read : A -&gt; B
}

forward : A -&gt; B where Read&lt;A, B&gt;
forward value = read value

newtype Token = Token(Int)

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = {
    with Read&lt;Token, Int&gt; {
        read (Token(value)) = value
    }
    forward (Token(42))
}
Identify the selection key in Read&lt;Token, Int&gt;, then locate the declaration that owns its nominal identity. Does that declaration supply an active local nominal? Predict which source occurrence the checker will mark:
$ ash --message-format human --locale en 05-no-local-key.ash
This evidence declaration needs an active local nominal type in its selection key: the determinant arguments, or all arguments when there is no determination edge.
  --&gt; /path/to/ash-doc/content/libraries/supplying-scoped-evidence/src/05-no-local-key.ash:12:10
12 |     with Read&lt;Token, Int&gt; {
   |          ^ This evidence declaration needs an active local nominal type in its selection key: the determinant arguments, or all arguments when there is no determination edge.
reason: evidence.missing-local-key
The diagnostic marks the exact application and states which positions form its key. This Token is a module nominal, not an active local declaration. Move the newtype back inside the block for the original scoped behavior. For behavior belonging to a module type, use an ordinary implementation in an owning module, following the preceding lessons.
with does not bypass module ownership or coherence rules. Abstract evidence from a function’s declared requirement and exact current-implementation evidence retain precedence over lexical evidence. Lexical evidence precedes matching concrete implementations; it does not rewrite those contracts.

Availability follows the occurrence’s source scope
The evidence source applies to the following remainder, including function bodies defined there. Compare that scope with this call’s position:
interface Read&lt;A, B&gt; where A -&gt; B {
    read : A -&gt; B
}

forward : A -&gt; B where Read&lt;A, B&gt;
forward value = read value

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = {
    newtype Token = Token(Int)
    let token = Token(40)
    let before = forward token
    with Read&lt;Token, Int&gt; {
        read (Token(value)) = value + 2
    }
    before
}
At forward token, which evidence declarations are already available? Predict whether the later with can supply this occurrence, then run the source:
$ ash --message-format human --locale en 06-before-evidence.ash
No available evidence satisfies this interface requirement.
  --&gt; /path/to/ash-doc/content/libraries/supplying-scoped-evidence/src/06-before-evidence.ash:12:18
12 |     let before = forward token
   |                  ^ No available evidence satisfies this interface requirement.
  --&gt; /path/to/ash-doc/content/libraries/supplying-scoped-evidence/src/06-before-evidence.ash:5:1
5 | forward : A -&gt; B where Read&lt;A, B&gt;
  | ^ This declaration introduced the interface requirement.
  --&gt; /path/to/ash-doc/content/libraries/supplying-scoped-evidence/src/06-before-evidence.ash:1:1
1 | interface Read&lt;A, B&gt; where A -&gt; B {
  | ^ This interface participates in the failing evidence selection.
2 |     read : A -&gt; B
  | ^ This interface participates in the failing evidence selection.
3 | }
  | ^ This interface participates in the failing evidence selection.
reason: constraint.unsatisfied
The call in before is outside the following remainder. Its labels lead back to forward’s required relation. The final before expression and main’s Int result annotation fix the complete requirement to Read&lt;Token, Int&gt;. What remains missing is evidence available at the call’s original position. Moving the evidence declaration before that call supplies the source. The compiler preserves an occurrence’s original lexical scope even when it finishes inference later.
Supplied method bodies can capture preceding ordinary values and use preceding evidence. The new source is not available inside its own supplied bodies, so it grants no implicit recursive or sibling method selection. If a method needs a recursive helper, define an appropriately annotated local recursive function earlier, using the rules from Learn.

Separate scopes can supply different methods
Two simultaneously active sources cannot supply the same key:
interface Read&lt;A, B&gt; where A -&gt; B {
    read : A -&gt; B
}

forward : A -&gt; B where Read&lt;A, B&gt;
forward value = read value

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = {
    newtype Token = Token(Int)
    with Read&lt;Token, Int&gt; {
        read (Token(value)) = value + 1
    }
    with Read&lt;Token, Int&gt; {
        read (Token(value)) = value + 2
    }
    forward (Token(40))
}
Mark the remainder governed by each with. Which earlier source remains active when the checker reaches the second declaration? Compare their keys to predict which two locations the checker needs to relate:
$ ash --message-format human --locale en 07-duplicate-evidence.ash
An active evidence declaration already supplies this exact interface application.
  --&gt; /path/to/ash-doc/content/libraries/supplying-scoped-evidence/src/07-duplicate-evidence.ash:14:10
14 |     with Read&lt;Token, Int&gt; {
   |          ^ An active evidence declaration already supplies this exact interface application.
  --&gt; /path/to/ash-doc/content/libraries/supplying-scoped-evidence/src/07-duplicate-evidence.ash:11:10
11 |     with Read&lt;Token, Int&gt; {
   |          ^ The earlier active evidence declaration is here.
reason: evidence.duplicate
The two labels identify the overlapping declarations. The second does not shadow the first. Remove one source when a single interpretation is intended. For two independent calculations with different interpretations, use disjoint blocks:
interface Read&lt;A, B&gt; where A -&gt; B {
    read : A -&gt; B
}

forward : A -&gt; B where Read&lt;A, B&gt;
forward value = read value

main : Unit -&gt; (Int, Int)
main () = {
    newtype Token = Token(Int)
    let token = Token(40)
    let first = {
        with Read&lt;Token, Int&gt; {
            read (Token(value)) = value + 1
        }
        forward token
    }
    let second = {
        with Read&lt;Token, Int&gt; {
            read (Token(value)) = value + 2
        }
        forward token
    }
    (first, second)
}
Follow each call using only the with source active in its block. Predict the pair’s values and explain why one block’s method cannot change the other:
$ ash 08-disjoint-scopes.ash
(41, 42) : (Int, Int)
The first method computes 40 + 1 and the second computes 40 + 2, giving (41, 42). Token remains in scope around both calculations, but each with applies only inside its own following remainder. Neither source becomes available to a new call after its block.
For a determined interface, simultaneously active equal keys with different dependent result types also conflict. Distinct local declarations remain distinct nominal keys even if they use the same representation.

Retain a selected method in an outward function
Lexical availability of new calls is separate from retaining a method already selected for a closure:
interface Read&lt;A, B&gt; where A -&gt; B {
    read : A -&gt; B
}

forward : A -&gt; B where Read&lt;A, B&gt;
forward value = read value

make_reader : Int -&gt; Unit -&gt; Int
make_reader amount = {
    newtype Token = Token(Int)
    let token = Token(amount)
    let offset = 2
    with Read&lt;Token, Int&gt; {
        read (Token(value)) = value + offset
    }
    fn(ignored) {
        forward token
    }
}

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = make_reader 40 ()
The function body occurs in the remainder after with. Use that source scope to identify the method selected for forward token, then trace the values it needs when called. Predict the result and check whether the returned Unit -&gt; Int type exposes Token:
$ ash 09-retained-method.ash
42 : Int
The selected method adds its captured two to the token’s saved 40, producing 42. The checked closure retains the method function, its offset capture, and the local token needed for the call. Its outward type exposes only Unit -&gt; Int.
After make_reader returns, calling this closure uses those retained ordinary functions and values. It does not search the caller’s block for evidence or export a new evidence fact that other calls can select.
The scoped evidence rules distinguish selecting in a lexical scope from retaining the selected functions. Local nominals and exact evidence add no acquisition, resource release, or permission to export resource access. Those separate obligations belong to the effects and resources path.

Request the bonus
In 02-handled.ash, score receives only the points. It requests the bonus when it needs to add it:
effect Bonus {
    amount : Unit -&gt; Int
}

score : Int -&gt; Int ! {Bonus}
score points = points + (perform Bonus.amount ())

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = handle (score 40) with Bonus {
    amount ignored =&gt; resume 2
}
The declaration effect Bonus names an effect API. Its one operation, amount : Unit -&gt; Int, accepts () and produces an integer. The declaration describes the request; it does not choose the integer that answers it.
In score, perform Bonus.amount () requests the missing bonus. The () supplies the operation’s Unit input. The addition needs an integer answer before it can finish. A request uses perform; declaring the operation does not give it an ordinary function body to call.
The signature score : Int -&gt; Int ! {Bonus} records both parts of the calculation’s contract. Calling score takes an integer and returns an integer, and it may request operations from Bonus. The part after ! is an effect row. {Bonus} is a closed row containing that one effect: it has no variable standing for further effects. It names the required effect API, not the value of the bonus or a particular handler.
The row tells a caller what must be accounted for when running the calculation. It supplies no answer itself. Follow the handler to see where the bonus comes from.

Change the answer, keep the calculation
Suppose another round grants a bonus of five points. In 03-different-bonus.ash, only the handler’s answer changes:
effect Bonus {
    amount : Unit -&gt; Int
}

score : Int -&gt; Int ! {Bonus}
score points = points + (perform Bonus.amount ())

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = handle (score 40) with Bonus {
    amount ignored =&gt; resume 5
}
Compare this file with the preceding one. The effect declaration, score signature, and score definition are unchanged. Follow the request with the new answer and predict the result, then run it:
$ ash 03-different-bonus.ash
45 : Int
The calculation still asks for an integer and adds it to the points. The handler now supplies 5, so the resumed addition produces 45. score does not select the handler or contain either bonus value.

effect Bonus {
    amount : Unit -&gt; Int
}

score : Int -&gt; Int ! {Bonus}
score points = points + (perform Bonus.amount ())

round_score : Int -&gt; Int ! {Bonus}
round_score points = score points

total : Int -&gt; Int ! {Bonus}
total points = round_score points

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = handle (total 40) with Bonus {
    amount ignored =&gt; resume 2
}


$ ash 01-call-chain.ash
42 : Int

Make a function that requests the bonus later
Suppose we want to remember the points now and calculate the score later. 02-request-later.ash returns a function that keeps those points:
effect Bonus {
    amount : Unit -&gt; Int
}

make_score : Int -&gt; Unit -&gt; Int ! {Bonus}
make_score points = fn(ignored) {
    points + (perform Bonus.amount ())
}

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = {
    let calculate = make_score 40
    handle (calculate ()) with Bonus {
        amount ignored =&gt; resume 2
    }
}
make_score 40 and calculate () are separate calls. Follow the body of make_score: it returns an anonymous function. Which call reaches the perform expression? Trace the request from that point to its handler, then predict the score:
$ ash 02-request-later.ash
42 : Int
Calling make_score 40 creates a function that keeps the points. It does not run the anonymous function’s body, so it does not request a bonus yet. The local name calculate holds that returned function. Calling calculate () runs the body and makes the request; that is the call enclosed by the handler. The clause supplies 2 for the bonus, so the addition produces 42.
Read Int -&gt; Unit -&gt; Int ! {Bonus} from the outside inward. The first call takes an Int and purely returns a function. That function takes Unit, returns an Int, and may request Bonus. The effect is latent: it belongs to a future call of the returned function.
The function value has a type describing that later call. The computation that creates the value has its own effects—in this case, none. Creating an anonymous function is pure even when its body contains a request.
The row’s placement explains why make_score 40 can appear before the handler. Moving the request into a function body changes when the request happens.

Request the bonus before returning a function
In 03-request-now.ash, we instead obtain the bonus while preparing the score. The returned function remembers both ordinary integers:
effect Bonus {
    amount : Unit -&gt; Int
}

prepare_score : Int -&gt; (Unit -&gt; Int) ! {Bonus}
prepare_score points = {
    let bonus = perform Bonus.amount ()
    fn(ignored) {
        points + bonus
    }
}

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = {
    let calculate = handle (prepare_score 40) with Bonus {
        amount ignored =&gt; resume 2
    }
    calculate ()
}
Follow the initializer of calculate before moving to the final call. Does the request happen before or after prepare_score 40 returns its function? Trace where the bonus is supplied and what the returned function keeps, then predict the result:
$ ash 03-request-now.ash
42 : Int
The block first requests the bonus and binds its answer to bonus. Only then does it create the anonymous function. The handler therefore surrounds prepare_score 40. Once that call returns, calculate () adds the captured integers without making another request. It produces the same 42 as the preceding program, through a different sequence of work.
Compare the two signatures:



Signature
First call
Returned function’s call




Int -&gt; Unit -&gt; Int ! {Bonus}
Pure
May request Bonus


Int -&gt; (Unit -&gt; Int) ! {Bonus}
May request Bonus
Pure



Arrows associate to the right. Without the parentheses, the row attaches to the innermost result computation. With them, the result of the first call is the whole pure function type Unit -&gt; Int, and ! {Bonus} describes producing that result. The matching scores do not make the contracts interchangeable: one handler surrounds the call that constructs the function, and the other surrounds the later call of the returned function.
The computation-type rules explain how rows attach to arrows. The function rules distinguish creating a function from applying it. The application rules describe when its stored body runs. When checking a call, Ash accounts for evaluating the function expression, evaluating its argument, and running the function body. Putting an effectful calculation in an argument does not hide its requirement either.

Give the row a parameter
03-open-row.ash makes both the value type and the effect row parameters:
effect Bonus {
    amount : Unit -&gt; Int
}

score : Int -&gt; Int ! {Bonus}
score points = points + (perform Bonus.amount ())

apply_twice&lt;A : Type, R : EffectRow&gt; : (A -&gt; A ! {| R}, A) -&gt; A ! {| R}
apply_twice (function, value) = function (function value)

increment : Int -&gt; Int
increment value = value + 1

main : Unit -&gt; (Int, Int)
main () = {
    let pure_score = apply_twice (increment, 40)
    let bonus_score = handle (apply_twice (score, 40)) with Bonus {
        amount ignored =&gt; resume 2
    }
    (pure_score, bonus_score)
}
A : Type says that A stands for an ordinary value type. R : EffectRow says that R stands for an effect row. A row parameter is not an integer, a handler, or an ordinary type parameter. It describes which effects a computation may require.
The notation {| R} is an open row: it has no explicitly listed effect instances and has the tail R. Both occurrences of {| R} in the signature refer to the same parameter. The callback may require R, so calling apply_twice may require R too. The helper calls the callback without installing a handler or adding a separate effect.
Each use of this signed global definition instantiates its parameters. For each call in main, identify A from the callback’s input and result types, and R from its effect row. Use those substitutions to trace the callback’s two calls, then predict both results:
$ ash 03-open-row.ash
(42, 44) : (Int, Int)
In the call with increment, A is Int and R is the empty row. In the call with score, A is again Int and R is {Bonus}. Each call gets the contract its callback needs. The second call is handled; the first needs no handler.
The effectful calculation adds two to 40, then adds two to 42. Calling the callback twice still requires the row {Bonus}, not two entries for Bonus. A row records effect requirements, not request counts or execution order. The inner call in function (function value) finishes before its result is passed to the outer call; the expression determines that order.
Compare the body of apply_twice with the earlier version. Only its signature needed to change. The row parameter lets that same body describe both uses. All the polymorphic helpers here have global signatures; this explanation does not depend on generalizing local function bindings.

effect Bonus {
    amount : Unit -&gt; Int
}

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = handle {
    let local_bonus = handle (perform Bonus.amount ()) with Bonus {
        amount ignored =&gt; resume 2
    }
    local_bonus + (perform Bonus.amount ())
} with Bonus {
    amount ignored =&gt; resume 10
}

