A clause can ask the surrounding handler
A local rule may build on an outer answer instead of replacing it with a constant. 02-forwarding.ash moves the second request into the inner clause:
effect Bonus {
    amount : Unit -&gt; Int
}

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = handle {
    handle (perform Bonus.amount ()) with Bonus {
        amount ignored =&gt; {
            let base = perform Bonus.amount ()
            resume (base + 2)
        }
    }
} with Bonus {
    amount ignored =&gt; resume 10
}
A clause runs outside the handler that selected it. Here, its request reaches the handlers surrounding the inner handler. With that boundary in mind, follow how base gets its value and predict the answer passed to the inner resume:
$ ash 02-forwarding.ash
12 : Int
The request in the inner body selects the inner clause. The request made directly by that clause goes past its own handler to the outer handler. It receives 10, then the inner clause resumes its suspended calculation with 12. The clause does not repeatedly select itself.
Both programs produce 12. The preceding one adds the answers to two body requests. This one uses the outer answer to construct the inner clause’s answer. The same final value can follow different request paths.
Handling removes the targeted requirement from the body. A request introduced by a clause remains an outward requirement until another handler answers it. Here the outer handler handles that requirement. The effect rules describe both boundaries.

Type arguments are part of the target
An operation API can be parameterized. In 03-instances.ash, Ask&lt;A&gt; requests a value of type A:
effect Ask&lt;A&gt; {
    value : Unit -&gt; A
}

main : Unit -&gt; (Int, Bool)
main () = {
    let points = handle (perform Ask&lt;Int&gt;.value ()) with Ask&lt;Int&gt; {
        value ignored =&gt; resume 40
    }
    let enabled = handle (perform Ask&lt;Bool&gt;.value ()) with Ask&lt;Bool&gt; {
        value ignored =&gt; resume true
    }
    (points, enabled)
}
Ask&lt;Int&gt; and Ask&lt;Bool&gt; are different instances of one declaration. Their operations return different value types. Match each requested instance to its handler, then predict the value and type of each tuple field:
$ ash 03-instances.ash
(40, true) : (Int, Bool)
Each handler targets exactly the instance requested in its body. The integer request receives 40, and the Boolean request receives true. Their operation result types determine what each clause may supply through resume.
Changing only the handler’s target does not redirect the request. Consider 04-wrong-instance.ash:
effect Ask&lt;A&gt; {
    value : Unit -&gt; A
}

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = handle (perform Ask&lt;Int&gt;.value ()) with Ask&lt;Bool&gt; {
    value ignored =&gt; resume false
}
Compare the type arguments on the request and the handler target before looking at the clause. Does the handler target the instance established by its body? Run the file to inspect that boundary:
$ ash --message-format human --locale en 04-wrong-instance.ash
