  |                                                   ^ The handled computation must explicitly request this exact effect instance, including its type arguments.
reason: effect.handler-missing-input
The label identifies Ask&lt;Bool&gt;, but the body requests Ask&lt;Int&gt;. The handler must target that exact instance. Restore the target to Ask&lt;Int&gt; and supply an integer through resume, as in the first binding of 03-instances.ash. Changing the target also changes the operation’s answer type, so the Boolean answer must change too. Matching operation names alone is insufficient.

A declaration keeps its identity across imports
Two modules can use the same spelling for independent APIs. The sibling files left.ash and right.ash each declare a public effect:
pub effect Bonus {
    amount : Unit -&gt; Int
}
pub effect Bonus {
    amount : Unit -&gt; Int
}
Both declarations are public, so their APIs are accessible through complete imports. The consumer 05-module-identities.ash qualifies each one:
import left as Left
import right as Right

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = handle {
    let first = handle (perform Left.Bonus.amount ()) with Left.Bonus {
        amount ignored =&gt; resume 2
    }
    first + (perform Right.Bonus.amount ())
} with Right.Bonus {
    amount ignored =&gt; resume 5
}
For each request, follow its import alias to the declaring module. Locate the handler that targets that declaration, then predict the sum from the two answers:
$ ash 05-module-identities.ash
7 : Int
The outer Right.Bonus handler does not answer the Left.Bonus request. The inner handler supplies 2 for the left declaration and the outer supplies 5 for the right. The calculation adds those answers to produce 7.
Qualification identifies the declaring module. Renaming an import alias does not create a new effect; declaring another effect does. The effect identity rules preserve this distinction even when spellings and operation signatures agree.

A closure keeps values, not its creating handler
Following effect contracts distinguished creating a function from calling it. In 06-returned-function.ash, creation itself includes one handled request:
effect Bonus {
    amount : Unit -&gt; Int
}

make_request : Unit -&gt; Unit -&gt; Int ! {Bonus}
make_request ignored = handle {
    let initial = perform Bonus.amount ()
    let request : Unit -&gt; Int ! {Bonus} = fn(ignored) {
        initial + (perform Bonus.amount ())
    }
    request
} with Bonus {
    amount ignored =&gt; resume 2
}

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = {
    let request = make_request ()
    handle (request ()) with Bonus {
        amount ignored =&gt; resume 9
    }
}
Separate the creating call, make_request (), from the later call, request (). Trace the first request while the function is being made, then identify which handler surrounds the request inside its body when it is called later. Predict the result from the saved value and the new answer:
$ ash 06-returned-function.ash
11 : Int
make_request () obtains 2 and saves it as initial. The local annotation on request gives the checker an expected function type. Its body has not run yet. Returning that closure retains initial, but does not retain the handler that supplied it.
Later, request () runs under a handler supplying 9. Its result combines the saved 2 with that new answer, producing 11.
There are two separate facts to trace: which ordinary values the closure captures, and which handler surrounds a request when the closure is called.

