Pass access to a helper
03-cell-count.ash gives a traversal helper the cell handle:
import @builtin.cell as Cell

type Scores = End
    | Item(Int, Scores)

visit : (Scores, Cell.Handle&lt;R&gt;) -&gt; Unit ! {Cell.Ops&lt;R&gt;} where Cell.Provider&lt;R&gt;
visit (End, counter) = ()
visit (Item(value, rest), counter) = {
    let before = Cell.read counter
    let ignored = Cell.write (counter, before + 1)
    visit (rest, counter)
}

main : Unit -&gt; Int
main () = resource cell&lt;R&gt; counter = 0 {
    let values = Item(20, Item(0, Item(99, End)))
    let ignored = visit (values, counter)
    Cell.read counter
}
visit returns Unit; its useful work is updating the cell. Before each recursive call, it reads the current count and writes one more. At End, it finishes without changing the count. Use the same invariant as the pure version: after each update, the count equals the number of items visited. Predict the outer body’s final read:
$ ash 03-cell-count.ash
3 : Int
Each of the three items causes one update, including zero, so the final read is 3. The cell evaluation rules give reads and writes access to the one payload owned by this acquisition.
Read the helper’s contract in two parts. ! {Cell.Ops&lt;R&gt;} records the effects of its reads and writes. where Cell.Provider&lt;R&gt; requires evidence permitting those methods for this handle identity. The parameter R connects the handle, its operations, and its provider requirement.
Acquisition supplies both that exact provider evidence and an interpreter for Cell.Ops&lt;R&gt; inside the body. This is why the call to visit can satisfy its requirement there, and why the enclosing main still has an empty row. Other effects introduced by a body would remain outward until separately handled.
The cell rules make this provider sealed. Importing names, selecting ordinary public implementations, or writing a source impl cannot manufacture cell authority. An ordinary helper can require and forward the authority supplied by an enclosing acquisition.

