State the operations we need
Work out the function types before filling their bodies. In the following type calculations, D abbreviates Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt; in the explanation; it is not a new Ash type or a declaration in the program. The source signatures keep the full spelling.
The public operation has the same job as before: insert a key and value into a valid dictionary. If the comparison is Equal, replace that association’s value; otherwise add the association. Return a valid dictionary with all other associations retained. We supply a key of type K, then a value of type V, then a dictionary of type D; the result must also have type D. Reading those inputs and the result in order gives K -&gt; V -&gt; D -&gt; D. Comparison needs Order&lt;K&gt;, so the signature includes that requirement. The following source signature is a non-executable excerpt:
pub insert : K -&gt; V -&gt; Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt; Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt; where Order&lt;K&gt;
Insertion still descends through one ordered branch. We need an internal operation that performs that descent while allowing a temporary red root or an edge between two red nodes. Its input is a recursively consistent subtree. Its result retains ordering and black-height, but may have a red-red edge directly below its red root. A black ancestor must repair that edge. The requirement on Order&lt;K&gt; supplies the comparison that selects the branch. Its job still takes a K, a V, and a D and returns a D, so insert_colored has the same function type as public insertion:
insert_colored : K -&gt; V -&gt; Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt; Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt; where Order&lt;K&gt;
When recursion returns a child, the parent has its color, its two branches, and its own association available. Carry the left dictionary and the parent’s key and value together, leaving the color and right dictionary as separate inputs. This small private type gives that group a name:
type RArg&lt;K, V&gt; = RArg(Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt;, K, V)
RArg means repair argument. Its constructor takes a dictionary, a key, and that key’s value. The dictionary lies immediately before the association in key order. Each group therefore describes one part of the ordered arrangement: a subtree followed by an association. The type groups those values; it does not establish their ordering or black-height conditions.
Rebuilding the parent now takes a Color, an RArg&lt;K, V&gt;, and a D, and returns a D. Those successive inputs calculate the signature of restore, which repairs a red-red edge beneath a black parent when it finds one:


restore : Color -&gt; RArg&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt; Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt; Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt;

That repair rearranges three associations with four intervening subtrees; the rearrangement itself is the literature’s rotation. Three RArg values carry the first three subtrees and their following associations; one final D supplies the rightmost subtree. All four inputs produce one D. The different orientations will supply these same groups to one construction:
restore_three : (
    RArg&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt; RArg&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt; RArg&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt;
    Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt; Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt;
)
The arrows still describe successive calls. A constructed RArg is one argument to a call, so it has grouping parentheses when passed to another function. Its fields have already been collected into one value, like the tuple inputs from Learn.
Finally, internal insertion may return a red root. blacken_root restores the public root condition without searching the tree or changing an association. It receives one whole dictionary and returns one whole dictionary, giving D -&gt; D:
blacken_root : Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt; Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt;
We can already check how these operations fit together. Once we supply key : K and value : V, insert_colored key value has remaining type D -&gt; D. Give it a dictionary and its result has type D. blacken_root accepts that result because its input type is also D. Its output is D, which is exactly what public insertion promises. We have calculated the connection between the two operations before choosing either body. Ash checks application inputs and results under its function and application typing rules.
These signatures describe the shapes of inputs and outputs. The purposes add requirements that the types do not express. Completed dictionaries and temporary insertion results have the same Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt; type; their stated conditions differ. In particular, restore is a local repair for candidates produced by insertion. It is not a general operation for correcting an arbitrary malformed tree.

Determine the operation types
Start with the caller’s two operations. Preparing a dictionary gives an iterator with the same key and value types:
pub iterator : Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt; Iterator&lt;K, V&gt;
Advancing it either reports completion or returns one association together with the state for the next request:


pub next : Iterator&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt; Option&lt;((K, V), Iterator&lt;K, V&gt;)&gt;

Read the successful payload from the outside in. Some holds a pair whose first component is (K, V) and whose second is Iterator&lt;K, V&gt;. None has no payload. Keeping the later iterator is what makes another request possible; keeping the current one allows a caller to repeat the same request.
These operations need no Order&lt;K&gt; requirement. Insertion has already established the tree’s ordering; traversal follows its structure. It neither compares keys nor repairs a malformed ordering.
One private operation must prepare the next frame. It takes a subtree and an existing stack of later ancestors, then returns the prepared stack:


push_left : Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt; FrameStack&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt; FrameStack&lt;K, V&gt;

The second input matters when resuming: after visiting a saved right subtree, traversal still owes the associations recorded in the remaining stack. Starting each descent with NoFrame would discard those ancestors.

Predict one transfer
The first entry imports the transparent support constructors and the three queue operations through the qualified Queues namespace, following the pinned import and namespace rules. Its drain function repeatedly uses only the successor returned by dequeue.
Before running it, follow the representation:

Removing from empty_queue produces None.
Enqueuing 1, then 2, then 3 keeps the front at End and stores 3, 2, 1 in the rear.
Removing from that retained ready state reverses the rear, returns 1, and leaves 2, 3 in the front with an empty rear.
Draining that returned later state produces 2, 3 without another rear transfer.

import queues as Queues

drain : Queues.Queue&lt;A&gt; -&gt; Queues.List&lt;A&gt;
drain queue = match Queues.dequeue queue {
    Queues.None =&gt; Queues.End
    Queues.Some((first, later)) =&gt; Queues.More(first, drain later)
}

main : Unit -&gt; (
    Queues.Option&lt;(Int, Queues.Queue&lt;Int&gt;)&gt;,
    Queues.Queue&lt;Int&gt;,
    Int,
    Queues.Queue&lt;Int&gt;,
    Queues.List&lt;Int&gt;
)
main () = {
    let empty_queue = Queues.empty ()
    let ready = Queues.enqueue 3 (
        Queues.enqueue 2 (
            Queues.enqueue 1 empty_queue
        )
    )
    let empty_result = Queues.dequeue empty_queue
    match Queues.dequeue ready {
        Queues.None =&gt; (empty_result, ready, 0, ready, Queues.End)
        Queues.Some((first, later)) =&gt; (
            empty_result,
            ready,
            first,
            later,
            drain later
        )
    }
}
