The five result fields confirm the prediction in order: empty removal, pre-transfer state, oldest value, returned successor, and FIFO drain. Success uses one standard-output line and status 0 under the pinned ash contract.
The line spells Queue, More, and End because successful observation renders nominal data structurally. Opacity restricts constructor access in client source; it neither conceals structural output nor proves that the queue equations preserve the logical sequence.

Resume the same older queue twice
The ready binding remains in scope for both tuple elements under the pinned binding rules. The second entry retains one empty-front queue with the rear 3, 2, 1, then uses that same binding as the argument to two calls. Predict two identical results. Each call must independently reverse the complete rear, return 1, and produce a front containing 2, 3.
import queues as Queues

main : Unit -&gt; (
    Queues.Option&lt;(Int, Queues.Queue&lt;Int&gt;)&gt;,
    Queues.Option&lt;(Int, Queues.Queue&lt;Int&gt;)&gt;
)
main () = {
    let ready = Queues.enqueue 3 (
        Queues.enqueue 2 (
            Queues.enqueue 1 (Queues.empty ())
        )
    )
    (Queues.dequeue ready, Queues.dequeue ready)
}
Run the retained-state entry:
ash implementing-persistent-fifo-frontier/src/02-retained-state.ash
(Some(1, Queue(More(2, More(3, End)), End)), Some(1, Queue(More(2, More(3, End)), End))) : (Option&lt;(Int, Queue&lt;Int&gt;)&gt;, Option&lt;(Int, Queue&lt;Int&gt;)&gt;)
Both fields confirm that the older source value can produce the same structural result twice. They do not show pointer identity, physical sharing, allocation behavior, or memoization.
Retained branching repeats the transfer outside that linear-history bound.
More generally, b calls from one retained pre-transfer queue perform that rear transfer b times. The accounting assigned to one successor chain cannot pay for arbitrary additional futures in a branching history.
At source level, enqueue passes the old front unchanged and places the old rear under one new More. Ordinary removal passes the old front tail and rear into the successor. Transfer constructs a new front while the retained older state can still refer to its rear. These relationships explain persistence without claiming a runtime storage representation.

Derive one scheduling transition
Both searches mark the start in an empty dictionary before adding Work(start, End). Their pending and maximum fields start at Next(Zero), and expanded starts at 0.
After removing an item, the engine decrements pending before it tests the current vertex against the target. A match returns the forward path without expanding the target. A mismatch increments expanded and schedules the current vertex’s neighbors from list head to tail.
For each neighbor, schedule performs these steps in order:

Look it up in the current threaded visited dictionary.
If it is present, leave the state unchanged and continue with the rest.
If it is absent, insert it before constructing or adding its work.
Extend the reverse path with the current vertex.
Add the new work item to the frontier.
Increment pending, retain the greater of that value and maximum, and continue from the complete successor state.

Marking a vertex before adding its work prevents repeated scheduling; it does not by itself make a path shortest. 
The dictionary is a front-inserted list of entries. Its failed equality branch continues through the older entries, so a lookup observes the complete retained dictionary. The pinned match and coverage rules check the constructor cases. The pinned binding rules evaluate each initializer before its successor binding, which supports the source-order state derivation above.
Only the frontier operations vary. search receives an add function and a remove function over one type F. The pinned rank-1 global model instantiates that type at each wrapper. depth_first supplies stack_add and stack_remove over List&lt;Work&gt;. breadth_first supplies queue_add and queue_remove over Queue&lt;Work&gt;. The stack prepends and removes at the front. The queue prepends to its rear list and removes the earliest logical item from its front, transferring a reversed rear when necessary.
Here is the complete dependency module. Its public boundary contains the four public data types and two search wrappers. The support types and helpers remain private under the pinned declaration visibility rules.
pub type Vertex =
    Start
    | Short
    | LongOne
    | LongTwo
    | WideOne
    | WideTwo
    | Goal
    | Isolated

pub type List&lt;A&gt; =
    End
    | More(A, List&lt;A&gt;)

pub type Natural =
    Zero
    | Next(Natural)

pub type SearchResult =
    Missing(Int, Natural)
    | Found(List&lt;Vertex&gt;, Int, Natural)

type Option&lt;A&gt; =
    Absent
    | Present(A)

type Entry&lt;K, V&gt; =
    Entry(K, V)

type Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt; =
    NoEntries
    | MoreEntry(Entry&lt;K, V&gt;, Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt;)

type Work =
    Work(Vertex, List&lt;Vertex&gt;)

type SearchState&lt;F&gt; =
    SearchState(F, Dictionary&lt;Vertex, Unit&gt;, Natural, Natural, Int)

type Queue&lt;A&gt; =
    Queue(List&lt;A&gt;, List&lt;A&gt;)

same_vertex : Vertex -&gt; Vertex -&gt; Bool
same_vertex Start Start = true
same_vertex Short Short = true
same_vertex LongOne LongOne = true
same_vertex LongTwo LongTwo = true
same_vertex WideOne WideOne = true
same_vertex WideTwo WideTwo = true
same_vertex Goal Goal = true
same_vertex Isolated Isolated = true
same_vertex ignored_left ignored_right = false

neighbors : Vertex -&gt; List&lt;Vertex&gt;
neighbors Start = More(Short, More(LongOne, End))
neighbors Short = More(WideOne, More(WideTwo, More(Goal, End)))
neighbors LongOne = More(LongTwo, End)
neighbors LongTwo = More(Start, More(Goal, End))
neighbors WideOne = End
neighbors WideTwo = End
neighbors Goal = End
neighbors Isolated = End

empty_dictionary : Unit -&gt; Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt;
empty_dictionary () = NoEntries

insert_dictionary : K -&gt; V -&gt; Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt; Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt;
insert_dictionary key value dictionary = MoreEntry(Entry(key, value), dictionary)

lookup_dictionary : (K -&gt; K -&gt; Bool) -&gt; K -&gt; Dictionary&lt;K, V&gt; -&gt; Option&lt;V&gt;
lookup_dictionary same_key query NoEntries = Absent
lookup_dictionary same_key query MoreEntry(Entry(key, value), rest) = match same_key query key {
    true =&gt; Present(value)
    false =&gt; lookup_dictionary same_key query rest
}

stack_add : A -&gt; List&lt;A&gt; -&gt; List&lt;A&gt;
stack_add value stack = More(value, stack)

stack_remove : List&lt;A&gt; -&gt; Option&lt;(A, List&lt;A&gt;)&gt;
stack_remove End = Absent
stack_remove More(first, rest) = Present((first, rest))

queue_empty : Unit -&gt; Queue&lt;A&gt;
queue_empty () = Queue(End, End)

queue_add : A -&gt; Queue&lt;A&gt; -&gt; Queue&lt;A&gt;
queue_add value Queue(front, rear) = Queue(front, More(value, rear))

reverse_into : List&lt;A&gt; -&gt; List&lt;A&gt; -&gt; List&lt;A&gt;
reverse_into End reversed = reversed
reverse_into More(first, rest) reversed = {
    reverse_into rest (More(first, reversed))
}

queue_remove : Queue&lt;A&gt; -&gt; Option&lt;(A, Queue&lt;A&gt;)&gt;
queue_remove Queue(End, End) = Absent
queue_remove Queue(More(first, rest), rear) = {
    Present((first, Queue(rest, rear)))
}
queue_remove Queue(End, rear) = {
    let front = reverse_into rear End
    queue_remove (Queue(front, End))
}

decrement : Natural -&gt; Natural
decrement Zero = Zero
decrement Next(rest) = rest

greater : Natural -&gt; Natural -&gt; Bool
greater Zero ignored_right = false
greater Next(ignored_left) Zero = true
greater Next(left) Next(right) = greater left right

retain_greater : Natural -&gt; Natural -&gt; Natural
retain_greater candidate retained = match greater candidate retained {
    true =&gt; candidate
    false =&gt; retained
}

forward_path : Work -&gt; List&lt;Vertex&gt;
forward_path Work(current, predecessors) = {
    reverse_into predecessors (More(current, End))
}

schedule : (Work -&gt; F -&gt; F) -&gt; List&lt;Vertex&gt; -&gt; Work -&gt; SearchState&lt;F&gt; -&gt; SearchState&lt;F&gt;
schedule add End parent state = state
schedule add More(next, rest) parent state = match state {
    SearchState(frontier, visited, pending, maximum, expanded) =&gt; match lookup_dictionary same_vertex next visited {
        Present(ignored_value) =&gt; schedule add rest parent state
        Absent =&gt; {
            let next_visited = insert_dictionary next () visited
            match parent {
                Work(current, predecessors) =&gt; {
                    let next_work = Work(next, More(current, predecessors))
                    let next_frontier = add next_work frontier
                    let next_pending = Next(pending)
                    let next_maximum = retain_greater next_pending maximum
                    let next_state = SearchState(
                        next_frontier,
                        next_visited,
                        next_pending,
                        next_maximum,
                        expanded
                    )
                    schedule add rest parent next_state
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

continue_search : (Work -&gt; F -&gt; F) -&gt; (F -&gt; Option&lt;(Work, F)&gt;) -&gt; Vertex -&gt; Dictionary&lt;Vertex, Unit&gt; -&gt; Natural -&gt; Natural -&gt; Int -&gt; Option&lt;(Work, F)&gt; -&gt; SearchResult
continue_search add remove target visited pending maximum expanded Absent = Missing(expanded, maximum)
continue_search add remove target visited pending maximum expanded Present((Work(current, predecessors), later_frontier)) = {
    let later_pending = decrement pending
    match same_vertex current target {
        true =&gt; Found(forward_path (Work(current, predecessors)), expanded, maximum)
        false =&gt; {
            let next_state = SearchState(
                later_frontier,
                visited,
                later_pending,
                maximum,
                expanded + 1
            )
            let scheduled = schedule add (neighbors current) (Work(current, predecessors)) next_state
            search add remove target scheduled
        }
    }
}

search : (Work -&gt; F -&gt; F) -&gt; (F -&gt; Option&lt;(Work, F)&gt;) -&gt; Vertex -&gt; SearchState&lt;F&gt; -&gt; SearchResult
search add remove target SearchState(frontier, visited, pending, maximum, expanded) = {
    let removed = remove frontier
    continue_search add remove target visited pending maximum expanded removed
}

pub depth_first : Vertex -&gt; Vertex -&gt; SearchResult
depth_first start target = {
    let visited = insert_dictionary start () (empty_dictionary ())
    let initial_work = Work(start, End)
    let frontier = stack_add initial_work End
    let initial_size = Next(Zero)
    let initial_state = SearchState(frontier, visited, initial_size, initial_size, 0)
    search stack_add stack_remove target initial_state
}

pub breadth_first : Vertex -&gt; Vertex -&gt; SearchResult
breadth_first start target = {
    let visited = insert_dictionary start () (empty_dictionary ())
    let initial_work = Work(start, End)
    let frontier = queue_add initial_work (queue_empty ())
    let initial_size = Next(Zero)
    let initial_state = SearchState(frontier, visited, initial_size, initial_size, 0)
    search queue_add queue_remove target initial_state
}

A tree traversal can number leaves from left to right by carrying the next unused number. This Case follows scoped cells and the earlier tree traversal. The pure version returns both the rebuilt tree and the next unused number, passing the left result’s counter into the right traversal. The effectful version requests each number through Fresh.next; its handler owns a scoped counter.
The comparison is the same tree labeled from zero by both versions, then a fresh run starting at ten. Explicit left and right bindings preserve the visit order. The counter and its handle stay within number; the rebuilt tree contains only copied integers.
import @builtin.cell as Cell

type Tree = Leaf(Int)
    | Fork(Tree, Tree)

effect Fresh {
    next : Unit -&gt; Int
}

thread_labels : (Tree, Int) -&gt; (Tree, Int)
thread_labels (Leaf(value), next) = (Leaf(next), next + 1)
thread_labels (Fork(left, right), next) = {
    let (new_left, after_left) = thread_labels (left, next)
    let (new_right, after_right) = thread_labels (right, after_left)
    (Fork(new_left, new_right), after_right)
}

label : Tree -&gt; Tree ! {Fresh}
label (Leaf(value)) = Leaf(perform Fresh.next ())
label (Fork(left, right)) = {
    let new_left = label left
    let new_right = label right
    Fork(new_left, new_right)
}

number : (Tree, Int) -&gt; Tree
number (tree, start) = resource cell&lt;R&gt; counter = start {
    handle (label tree) with Fresh {
        next () =&gt; {
            let value = Cell.read counter
            let ignored = Cell.write (counter, value + 1)
            resume value
        }
    }
}

main : Unit -&gt; (Tree, Tree, Tree)
main () = {
    let tree = Fork(Leaf(99), Fork(Leaf(99), Leaf(99)))
    let (threaded, next) = thread_labels (tree, 0)
    (threaded, number (tree, 0), number (tree, 10))
}
Run the program from the Cases directory:
ash numbering-tree-leaves/src/main.ash
The result is:
(Fork(Leaf(0), Fork(Leaf(1), Leaf(2))), Fork(Leaf(0), Fork(Leaf(1), Leaf(2))), Fork(Leaf(10), Fork(Leaf(11), Leaf(12)))) : (Tree, Tree, Tree)
The first two trees agree: each traversal visits three leaves and assigns 0, 1, then 2. The final tree begins at 10 because each call to number acquires its own counter. The request changes how the next number is supplied; the explicit left and right bindings still determine traversal order.
Change the traversal to visit the right subtree first and predict which leaf receives each number. Then restore it. The next Case uses a handler to measure a traversal while preserving its result.

An order calculation needs prices and a discount policy. Those dependencies can be function arguments, as the earlier tree lookup accepted a comparator. Effects offer another arrangement: the calculation requests the behavior, and the enclosing handler supplies it. Calls between the request and handler carry Pricing in their effect rows without carrying the individual functions.
This example follows requesting a value. order_total prices a book and a pen, then asks for a discount on their combined price. Unavailable is an ordinary answer, so the calculation decides how to respond to it. The three handlers supply a regular price, a promotion for this particular bundle, and an unavailable pen.
From the Cases directory, enter supplying-order-prices/src. Read the program and predict the three quotes before running ash main.ash.
type Product = Book
    | Pen

type Quote = Priced(Int)
    | Unavailable

effect Pricing {
    price : Product -&gt; Quote
    discount : Int -&gt; Int
}

order_total : (Product, Product) -&gt; Quote ! {Pricing}
order_total (first, second) = match perform Pricing.price first {
    Unavailable =&gt; Unavailable
    Priced(left) =&gt; match perform Pricing.price second {
        Unavailable =&gt; Unavailable
        Priced(right) =&gt; Priced(perform Pricing.discount (left + right))
    }
}

regular_price : Product -&gt; Quote
regular_price Book = Priced(20)
regular_price Pen = Priced(3)

regular : Unit -&gt; Quote
regular () = handle (order_total (Book, Pen)) with Pricing {
    price product =&gt; resume (regular_price product)
    discount total =&gt; resume total
}

promotion : Unit -&gt; Quote
promotion () = handle (order_total (Book, Pen)) with Pricing {
    price product =&gt; resume (regular_price product)
    discount total =&gt; resume (match total == 23 {
        true =&gt; 21
        false =&gt; total
    })
}

unavailable : Unit -&gt; Quote
unavailable () = handle (order_total (Book, Pen)) with Pricing {
    price product =&gt; resume (match product {
        Book =&gt; Priced(20)
        Pen =&gt; Unavailable
    })
    discount total =&gt; resume total
}

main : Unit -&gt; (Quote, Quote, Quote)
main () = (regular (), promotion (), unavailable ())
$ ash main.ash
(Priced(23), Priced(21), Unavailable) : (Quote, Quote, Quote)
The same order_total produces Priced(23), Priced(21), and Unavailable. The promotion maps this bundle’s total of 23 to 21; other totals stay unchanged. The unavailable response follows the calculation’s ordinary match branch. It does not abandon a continuation or manufacture a compiler error.
Each handler supplies both operations and resumes with the requested answer. Its enclosing function has an empty effect row because it handles Pricing. This is dependency injection through a scoped handler: the calculation states what it needs, and the surrounding program selects the behavior. Fixed responses also make the calculation reproducible without an external service.
As an exercise, change only the promotion handler to supply a different pen price. Predict whether its bundle discount still applies. For a later extension, add a third product and decide explicitly how an unavailable item affects the order. A general checkout system is unnecessary for this comparison.
Continue with a scripted session, where the supplied behavior changes across successive requests.

A session remembers which commands are currently meaningful. Here Start opens a session, Send is accepted only while it is open, and Close ends the run. Keep those rules in the pure transition function. The session loop requests commands and publishes replies through Session.
Continue from supplying dependencies and resuming a calculation. The handler uses a scoped cell for the script position. The session’s domain state remains an explicit argument; effects need not turn every piece of state into mutable storage.
From the Cases directory, enter running-a-scripted-session/src. The first script starts before sending; the second sends twice without starting. Predict the replies, then run ash main.ash.
import @builtin.cell as Cell

type Command = Start
    | Send(Int)
    | Close

type State = Idle
    | Open

type Reply = Started
    | Accepted(Int)
    | Refused
    | Closed

type Replies = End
    | More(Reply, Replies)

effect Session {
    receive : Unit -&gt; Command
    reply : Reply -&gt; Unit
}

transition : (State, Command) -&gt; (State, Reply)
transition (state, Start) = (Open, Started)
transition (Idle, Send(value)) = (Idle, Refused)
transition (Open, Send(value)) = (Open, Accepted(value))
transition (state, Close) = (Idle, Closed)

session : State -&gt; Replies ! {Session}
session state = {
    let command = perform Session.receive ()
    let (next, response) = transition (state, command)
    let ignored = perform Session.reply response
    match command {
        Close =&gt; End
        other =&gt; session next
    }
}

script : (Bool, Int) -&gt; Command
script (start_first, index) = match index == 0 {
    true =&gt; match start_first {
        true =&gt; Start
        false =&gt; Send(7)
    }
    false =&gt; match index == 1 {
        true =&gt; Send(42)
        false =&gt; Close
    }
}

run : Bool -&gt; Replies
run start_first = resource cell&lt;R&gt; cursor = 0 {
    handle (session Idle) with Session {
        receive () =&gt; {
            let index = Cell.read cursor
            let ignored = Cell.write (cursor, index + 1)
            resume (script (start_first, index))
        }
        reply response =&gt; {
            let rest = resume ()
            More(response, rest)
        }
    }
}

main : Unit -&gt; (Replies, Replies)
main () = (run true, run false)
$ ash main.ash
(More(Started, More(Accepted(42), More(Closed, End))), More(Refused, More(Refused, More(Closed, End)))) : (Replies, Replies)
The two runs use the same transition rules and loop; only the supplied script changes. Supplying a calculation’s inputs from its enclosing handler this way is dependency injection, as in the pricing Case. Each run starts its own cursor at zero, and both scripts eventually supply Close.
In the reply clause, resume () runs the remaining session and returns its Replies. Prepending the current reply to that result retains chronological order. The handler builds the transcript without adding a reply accumulator to the session loop. Input and output here are finite data, with no terminal or network connection.
The protocol is checked by transition at runtime. It does not establish session types or prevent callers from constructing an out-of-order command. Likewise, termination here depends on the supplied scripts reaching Close; the effect declaration alone does not guarantee it.
For practice, change the second script to send, start, send, then close. Predict its transcript before editing the script function. Keeping transition pure also lets you examine individual command/state pairs separately.
Continue with bounding search work, where a handler decides whether a calculation may take another step.

A search can ask permission before expanding each nonempty node. This combines frontier search, scoped cells, and early stopping. search keeps a depth-first frontier; Work.expand separates that traversal from the policy deciding whether another expansion is allowed. Empty trees are skipped without consuming the budget.
The handler counts admitted expansions in its own cell. At the limit it consumes the continuation with discard and returns Exhausted. The earlier writes remain visible, so the result includes work already admitted. Exhaustion means the search was stopped; Missing means the frontier was fully examined. The finite acyclic tree needs no visited set. This policy assumes a nonnegative budget: the counter starts at zero and increases by one until it equals the limit.
import @builtin.cell as Cell

type Tree = Empty
    | Node(Int, Tree, Tree)

type Frontier = End
    | Pending(Tree, Frontier)

type Result = Found(Int)
    | Missing
    | Exhausted

effect Work {
    expand : Unit -&gt; Unit
}

search : (Frontier, Int) -&gt; Result ! {Work}
search (End, query) = Missing
search (Pending(Empty, rest), query) = search (rest, query)
search (Pending(Node(value, left, right), rest), query) = {
    let ignored = perform Work.expand ()
    match value == query {
        true =&gt; Found(value)
        false =&gt; search (Pending(left, Pending(right, rest)), query)
    }
}

run : (Tree, Int, Int) -&gt; (Result, Int)
run (tree, query, budget) = resource cell&lt;R&gt; used = 0 {
    let result = handle (search (Pending(tree, End), query)) with Work {
        expand () =&gt; match Cell.read used == budget {
            true =&gt; {
                let ignored = discard
                Exhausted
            }
            false =&gt; {
                let count = Cell.read used
                let ignored = Cell.write (used, count + 1)
                resume ()
            }
        }
    }
    (result, Cell.read used)
}

main : Unit -&gt; ((Result, Int), (Result, Int), (Result, Int), (Result, Int))
main () = {
    let tree = Node(10, Node(20, Empty, Empty), Node(30, Empty, Empty))
    (run (tree, 30, 0), run (tree, 30, 2),
        run (tree, 30, 3), run (tree, 99, 3))
}
Run the program from the Cases directory:
ash bounding-search-work/src/main.ash
The result is:
((Exhausted, 0), (Exhausted, 2), (Found(30), 3), (Missing, 3)) : ((Result, Int), (Result, Int), (Result, Int), (Result, Int))
The nonempty nodes are admitted in order 10, 20, 30. A zero budget stops before testing the first value; a budget of two admits the first two nodes but stops before the target. Three permits the target comparison. With the same budget, the absent query finishes the frontier and returns Missing. The count includes each admitted node even when it contains the answer.
Change the budget or child order and predict whether the target is reached before exhaustion. This policy limits expansions before they happen; the next Case changes stored links to reduce work on a later lookup.

Union–find represents a set by a root reached through parent links. This example follows native buffers and isolates its lookup step: four buffer entries describe the parent chain 3 → 2 → 1 → 0, with zero as its own parent. The buffer starts at zero; the two checked writes establish the remaining links. Valid parent links and an acyclic path to a self-parent are assumptions of this fixed input.
find_root requests parent access through Parents. With compression enabled, it writes the discovered root into every non-root entry on the return path; this rewrite toward the root is called path compression, and later lookups from those entries start nearer the root. The handler interprets those requests as checked scoped-buffer operations. Root records the root, reads, and writes for one lookup. Two lookups without compression should each read four entries and write none. With compression, the first should read four and write three; the second should read two and write one. Initialization is outside these counts, and the second run acquires its own buffer.
These source-operation counts illustrate the tradeoff for this chain. They make no timing or amortized-complexity claim. Every access failure propagates as ordinary Outcome data. The outer Outcome reports acquisition; the inner one reports initialization or lookup. Successful acquisition does not hide an access failure.
import @ffi.native_buffer as Buffer

type Lookup = Root(Int, Int, Int)

effect Parents {
    read : Int -&gt; Buffer.Outcome&lt;Int&gt;
    write : (Int, Int) -&gt; Buffer.Outcome&lt;Unit&gt;
}

record_root : (Int, Bool, Lookup) -&gt; Buffer.Outcome&lt;Lookup&gt; ! {Parents}
record_root (node, false, Root(root, reads, writes)) = Buffer.Success(Root(root, reads + 1, writes))
record_root (node, true, Root(root, reads, writes)) = {
    match perform Parents.write (node, root) {
        Buffer.Failure(error) =&gt; Buffer.Failure(error)
        Buffer.Success(()) =&gt; Buffer.Success(Root(root, reads + 1, writes + 1))
    }
}

find_root : (Int, Bool) -&gt; Buffer.Outcome&lt;Lookup&gt; ! {Parents}
find_root (node, compress) = match perform Parents.read node {
    Buffer.Failure(error) =&gt; Buffer.Failure(error)
    Buffer.Success(parent) =&gt; match parent == node {
        true =&gt; Buffer.Success(Root(node, 1, 0))
        false =&gt; match find_root (parent, compress) {
            Buffer.Failure(error) =&gt; Buffer.Failure(error)
            Buffer.Success(result) =&gt; record_root (node, compress, result)
        }
    }
}

repeat_lookup : Bool -&gt; Buffer.Outcome&lt;(Lookup, Lookup)&gt; ! {Parents}
repeat_lookup compress = match find_root (3, compress) {
    Buffer.Failure(error) =&gt; Buffer.Failure(error)
    Buffer.Success(first) =&gt; match find_root (3, compress) {
        Buffer.Failure(error) =&gt; Buffer.Failure(error)
        Buffer.Success(second) =&gt; Buffer.Success((first, second))
    }
}

run : Bool -&gt; Buffer.Outcome&lt;Buffer.Outcome&lt;(Lookup, Lookup)&gt;&gt;
run compress = resource Buffer.Buffer&lt;R&gt; parents = 4 {
    match Buffer.write (parents, 2, 1) {
        Buffer.Failure(error) =&gt; Buffer.Failure(error)
        Buffer.Success(()) =&gt; match Buffer.write (parents, 3, 2) {
            Buffer.Failure(error) =&gt; Buffer.Failure(error)
            Buffer.Success(()) =&gt; handle (repeat_lookup compress) with Parents {
                read index =&gt; resume (Buffer.read (parents, index))
                write pair =&gt; {
                    let (index, parent) = pair
                    resume (Buffer.write (parents, index, parent))
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

main : Unit -&gt; (Buffer.Outcome&lt;Buffer.Outcome&lt;(Lookup, Lookup)&gt;&gt;,
    Buffer.Outcome&lt;Buffer.Outcome&lt;(Lookup, Lookup)&gt;&gt;)
main () = (run false, run true)
