Select an imported policy
A policy token makes that choice part of an interface contract. In pricing.ash, PricePolicy&lt;P&gt; requires unit_price to accept both a token of type P and a product. The result is still an available unit price or Unavailable. The generic quote_order accepts the same token and an order:
import orders as Orders

pub interface PricePolicy&lt;P&gt; {
    unit_price : (P, Orders.Product) -&gt; Orders.Quote
}

pub quote_order : (P, Orders.Order) -&gt; Orders.Quote where PricePolicy&lt;P&gt;
quote_order (policy, order) = {
    let price = fn(product) {
        unit_price (policy, product)
    }
    Orders.quote_with (price, order)
}
The interface requirement supplies behavior for the complete application PricePolicy&lt;P&gt;. Inside quote_order, price retains the token and calls the required method with each product. It has the ordinary function shape that Orders.quote_with needs. The order itself still comes from the caller; the adapter changes how prices are supplied, not which order is priced.
base_policy.ash owns a public nominal token and its implementation:
import orders as Orders
pub import pricing (PricePolicy)
import pricing (unit_price)

pub newtype BasePolicy = BasePolicy(Unit)

pub impl PricePolicy&lt;BasePolicy&gt; {
    unit_price (BasePolicy(ignored), product) = Orders.base_price product
}
BasePolicy carries unit because this implementation needs no configuration. Its method ignores that unit and uses base_price. The public import exposes the existing PricePolicy identity through this module so clients can select its implementation. The private method import makes the unit_price spelling available for the implementation body.
The entry imports the operation and explicitly selects the public fact:
import orders as Orders
import pricing (quote_order)
import base_policy (BasePolicy, impl PricePolicy)

main : Unit -&gt; Orders.Quote
main () = quote_order (BasePolicy(()), Orders.sample_order ())
BasePolicy imports the type and its public constructor. The separate impl PricePolicy item selects implementation evidence; it binds no source name. Importing names alone does not select implementations. Follow the token to PricePolicy&lt;BasePolicy&gt;, then predict the quote:
$ ash supplying-a-local-pricing-policy/src/02-imported-policy.ash
Priced(4900) : Quote
The answer is again 4900 cents because the selected method supplies the same unit prices. Selection is checked before execution. The method then runs as an ordinary function, without a runtime policy registry.

Capture a tariff in a local method
An imported implementation has a module-owned policy type. A caller can instead introduce a local nominal key and supply exact behavior in a block, following scoped evidence. TariffPolicy below carries unit as well: the tariff belongs to the method’s ordinary capture, not the token payload.
import orders as Orders
import pricing (PricePolicy, unit_price, quote_order)

main : Unit -&gt; (Orders.Quote, Orders.Quote)
main () = {
    newtype TariffPolicy = TariffPolicy(Unit)
    let order = Orders.sample_order ()
    let first = {
        let tariff = 100
        with PricePolicy&lt;TariffPolicy&gt; {
            unit_price (TariffPolicy(ignored), product) = Orders.tariff_price tariff product
        }
        quote_order (TariffPolicy(()), order)
    }
    let second = {
        let tariff = 200
        with PricePolicy&lt;TariffPolicy&gt; {
            unit_price (TariffPolicy(ignored), product) = Orders.tariff_price tariff product
        }
        quote_order (TariffPolicy(()), order)
    }
    (first, second)
}
Each block binds its integer tariff before with. The supplied method can capture that preceding value. The declaration supplies the complete application PricePolicy&lt;TariffPolicy&gt; only in its following lexical remainder, which includes that block’s quote_order call. It is exact evidence rather than a local implementation search.
The two evidence sources have disjoint scopes. The second begins after the first block has returned, so they never compete for the same active key. Predict both amounts from the five items:
$ ash supplying-a-local-pricing-policy/src/03-local-policy.ash
(Priced(5400), Priced(5900)) : (Quote, Quote)
The second tariff adds 1000 cents to the base total: 2 * 2200 + 3 * 500 = 5900. The distinction between the blocks concerns which method a source occurrence selects. It does not add runtime nominal creation or a dynamically replaced global policy. The exact-evidence rules define the source scope and the ordinary captures separately.

Keep nested choices distinct
Nested choices need distinct active keys. Reusing the same complete key in two overlapping with declarations would supply duplicate evidence, rather than shadowing the outer source. This version introduces OuterPolicy and InnerPolicy:
import orders as Orders
import pricing (PricePolicy, unit_price, quote_order)

main : Unit -&gt; (Orders.Quote, Orders.Quote, Orders.Quote)
main () = {
    newtype OuterPolicy = OuterPolicy(Unit)
    let order = Orders.sample_order ()
    let tariff = 100
    with PricePolicy&lt;OuterPolicy&gt; {
        unit_price (OuterPolicy(ignored), product) = Orders.tariff_price tariff product
    }
    let before = quote_order (OuterPolicy(()), order)
    let inside = {
        newtype InnerPolicy = InnerPolicy(Unit)
        let tariff = 200
        with PricePolicy&lt;InnerPolicy&gt; {
            unit_price (InnerPolicy(ignored), product) = Orders.tariff_price tariff product
        }
        quote_order (InnerPolicy(()), order)
    }
    let after = quote_order (OuterPolicy(()), order)
    (before, inside, after)
}
The before and after calls both pass an OuterPolicy token and therefore need PricePolicy&lt;OuterPolicy&gt;. The inner call passes an InnerPolicy token and needs PricePolicy&lt;InnerPolicy&gt;. Both sources are active in the inner remainder, but their nominal keys differ. Predict which tariff each call uses:
$ ash supplying-a-local-pricing-policy/src/04-nested-policies.ash
(Priced(5400), Priced(5900), Priced(5400)) : (Quote, Quote, Quote)
The inner integer binding named tariff does not change the 100 already captured by the outer method. Leaving the inner block also leaves the outer source available to after. The result follows the call’s token type and lexical evidence, not the most recently written tariff value.

Return the quoting function
A caller may want to choose a tariff once and retain a function that prices later orders. The public contract needed for that task is Order -&gt; Quote; it does not need to mention the local policy type. make_pricer builds precisely that function:
import orders as Orders
import pricing (PricePolicy, unit_price, quote_order)

make_pricer : Int -&gt; (Orders.Order -&gt; Orders.Quote)
make_pricer tariff = {
    newtype TariffPolicy = TariffPolicy(Unit)
    let policy = TariffPolicy(())
    with PricePolicy&lt;TariffPolicy&gt; {
        unit_price (TariffPolicy(ignored), product) = Orders.tariff_price tariff product
    }
    fn(order) {
        quote_order (policy, order)
    }
}

main : Unit -&gt; (Orders.Quote, Orders.Quote, Orders.Quote)
main () = {
    let first = make_pricer 100
    let second = make_pricer 200
    let order = Orders.sample_order ()
    (first order, second order, first order)
}
The returned function’s body occurs after with, so quote_order selects PricePolicy&lt;TariffPolicy&gt; there. The checked closure retains the selected method function, its tariff capture, and the token needed by the call. The local nominal scope rule permits this result because its exposed Orders.Order -&gt; Orders.Quote type contains no local identity.
main constructs both quoting functions before calling either one. The two factory calls use the same lexical nominal declaration; their ordinary tariff captures differ. No runtime-fresh nominal identity is needed. Predict the three calls, including the repeated call to first after second exists:
$ ash supplying-a-local-pricing-policy/src/05-returned-closure.ash
(Priced(5400), Priced(5900), Priced(5400)) : (Quote, Quote, Quote)
Both evidence blocks have returned by the time these functions run. Each closure uses its retained method and values. Calling it does not select new evidence in the caller’s scope or export an evidence fact for other calls. This is the separation between lexical availability and retained behavior specified by the scoped exact evidence rules.

pub type EntityId = EntityId(Int)

pub type Position = Position(Int, Int)

pub type Velocity = Velocity(Int, Int)

pub type Option&lt;C&gt; = None
    | Some(C)

pub type Rows&lt;C&gt; = NoRows
    | Row(EntityId, C, Rows&lt;C&gt;)

pub opaque type Store&lt;C&gt; = Store(Rows&lt;C&gt;)

pub empty : Unit -&gt; Store&lt;C&gt;
empty () = Store(NoRows)

pub entries : Store&lt;C&gt; -&gt; Rows&lt;C&gt;
entries Store(rows) = rows

same_entity : (EntityId, EntityId) -&gt; Bool
same_entity (EntityId(left), EntityId(right)) = left == right

lookup_rows : (EntityId, Rows&lt;C&gt;) -&gt; Option&lt;C&gt;
lookup_rows (query, NoRows) = None
lookup_rows (query, Row(entity, component, rest)) = match same_entity(query, entity) {
    true =&gt; Some(component)
    false =&gt; lookup_rows(query, rest)
}

pub lookup : (EntityId, Store&lt;C&gt;) -&gt; Option&lt;C&gt;
lookup (query, Store(rows)) = lookup_rows(query, rows)

put_rows : (EntityId, C, Rows&lt;C&gt;) -&gt; Rows&lt;C&gt;
put_rows (entity, component, NoRows) = Row(entity, component, NoRows)
put_rows (entity, component, Row(current, saved, rest)) = match same_entity(entity, current) {
    true =&gt; Row(entity, component, rest)
    false =&gt; Row(current, saved, put_rows(entity, component, rest))
}

pub put : (EntityId, C, Store&lt;C&gt;) -&gt; Store&lt;C&gt;
put (entity, component, Store(rows)) = Store(put_rows(entity, component, rows))

Join identifiers before moving positions
A join walks the position rows. For each identifier it looks up a velocity. An absent velocity contributes no joined row; a present velocity contributes one row containing the original position and its matching velocity. Recursing on the remaining positions preserves their order. Since the position store is unique, the resulting identifiers are unique too.
Movement then receives the joined rows and the original position store. advance adds (dx, dy) to (x, y). move_positions puts that new position back under the joined identifier, carrying the returned store into the next recursive call. Every joined identifier already occurs in the position store, so these puts replace existing entries. Unjoined positions keep their values and their positions in the enumeration.
First keep access concrete. A World contains a position store followed by a velocity store. move_direct unpacks those two fields, joins them, moves the joined positions, and constructs World(moved, velocities). It keeps the velocity field from its input. The complete world module appears below when we add generic access; this entry uses its concrete operation:
import stores (Rows, Position, Velocity)
import systems (join_stores)
import world (World, initial, move_direct)

main : Unit -&gt; (Rows&lt;(Position, Velocity)&gt;, World, World)
main () = {
    let original = initial ()
    let World(positions, velocities) = original
    (join_stores(positions, velocities), move_direct original, original)
}
Run commands from content/cases/ throughout this chapter:
ash querying-an-immutable-world/src/01-direct-movement.ash
The result is one tuple: the joined rows, the moved world, and the retained original world. Its type is (Rows&lt;(Position, Velocity)&gt;, World, World). The first field of that captured result is:
Row(EntityId(1), (Position(10, 5), Velocity(2, -1)), Row(EntityId(3), (Position(30, 9), Velocity(1, 2)), NoRows))
This is one complete field, with the surrounding tuple and worlds omitted. The moved world’s positions are 1=(12,4), 2=(20,7), and 3=(31,11). For example, entity 1 computes (10+2, 5+(-1)) = (12,4). The velocity store still enumerates 3, 1, 4 with their original values. The third field still has positions 1=(10,5), 2=(20,7), 3=(30,9). The checks compare the complete stores, including their order and every component, rather than an aggregate coordinate sum.

State which store the system needs
The concrete operation fixes the world’s representation. A generic movement system can instead require two operations: read a component store and replace that store while keeping the rest of the world. Continue the implementation-evidence lesson by giving that requirement two parameters, StoreAccess&lt;W, C&gt;.
Its component_store method has type W -&gt; Store&lt;C&gt;. Its replace_store method has type (W, Store&lt;C&gt;) -&gt; W. Both parameters occur in each method’s value type, as the interface rules require for an interface without a determination edge. The complete application is the evidence key. For this world we need both StoreAccess&lt;World, Position&gt; and StoreAccess&lt;World, Velocity&gt;.
A determination edge from W to C would say that a world type determines one component type. Our world has two stores, so that would describe the wrong relationship. Keep both arguments in the key and use ordinary type context to fix the requested component. In joined and move_world, the local result annotations distinguish the two reads of component_store. The final replace_store receives a Store&lt;Position&gt;; its argument fixes the same component choice.
Here is the system module. It imports storage types and operations and has no import of the concrete world:
import stores (EntityId, Position, Velocity, Store, Rows, NoRows, Row, None, Some, entries, lookup, put)

pub interface StoreAccess&lt;W, C&gt; {
    component_store : W -&gt; Store&lt;C&gt;
    replace_store : (W, Store&lt;C&gt;) -&gt; W
}

join_rows : (Rows&lt;Position&gt;, Store&lt;Velocity&gt;) -&gt; Rows&lt;(Position, Velocity)&gt;
join_rows (NoRows, velocities) = NoRows
join_rows (Row(entity, position, rest), velocities) = match lookup(entity, velocities) {
    None =&gt; join_rows(rest, velocities)
    Some(velocity) =&gt; Row(entity, (position, velocity), join_rows(rest, velocities))
}

pub join_stores : (Store&lt;Position&gt;, Store&lt;Velocity&gt;) -&gt; Rows&lt;(Position, Velocity)&gt;
join_stores (positions, velocities) = join_rows(entries positions, velocities)

pub advance : (Position, Velocity) -&gt; Position
advance (Position(x, y), Velocity(dx, dy)) = Position(x + dx, y + dy)

pub move_positions : (Rows&lt;(Position, Velocity)&gt;, Store&lt;Position&gt;) -&gt; Store&lt;Position&gt;
move_positions (NoRows, positions) = positions
move_positions (Row(entity, pair, rest), positions) = {
    let updated = put(entity, advance pair, positions)
    move_positions(rest, updated)
}

pub joined&lt;W&gt; : W -&gt; Rows&lt;(Position, Velocity)&gt; where StoreAccess&lt;W, Position&gt;, StoreAccess&lt;W, Velocity&gt;
joined world = {
    let positions : Store&lt;Position&gt; = component_store world
    let velocities : Store&lt;Velocity&gt; = component_store world
    join_stores(positions, velocities)
}

pub move_world&lt;W&gt; : W -&gt; W where StoreAccess&lt;W, Position&gt;, StoreAccess&lt;W, Velocity&gt;
move_world world = {
    let positions : Store&lt;Position&gt; = component_store world
    let velocities : Store&lt;Velocity&gt; = component_store world
    let moved = move_positions(join_stores(positions, velocities), positions)
    replace_store(world, moved)
}
The two where requirements let each generic body use the corresponding methods as abstract evidence. When a caller fixes W to World, it must supply the two complete applications. Constraint satisfaction selects that evidence; importing a method or constrained function name alone does not select an implementation.
move_world finishes all position updates before one call to replace_store. That is one replacement of the world’s position field. Each put inside move_positions still searches and reconstructs part of the association list; we have not made all that work disappear.
The world module re-exports the interface name and publishes two implementation facts. Each implementation reads one field and replaces only that field. The direct operation uses the same join and position-update calculation, with its field access written explicitly.
The fixed velocity for entity 1 needs the integer -1. Ordinary expression syntax has no negative literal or subtraction operator at this checkpoint. The private constant-only declaration binds DecreaseOne to that ordinary Int through the existing passive scalar constant form. It declares no symbol and executes no native call. The world uses that value only to construct Velocity(2, DecreaseOne):
import stores (EntityId, Position, Velocity, Store, empty, put)
pub import systems (StoreAccess)
import systems (component_store, replace_store, join_stores, move_positions)

foreign library FixtureNumbers {
    profile "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu"
    repr Signed = signed(64)
    const DecreaseOne : Signed = -1
}

pub type World = World(Store&lt;Position&gt;, Store&lt;Velocity&gt;)

pub initial : Unit -&gt; World
initial () = {
    let positions = put(EntityId(1), Position(10, 5), empty())
    let positions = put(EntityId(2), Position(20, 7), positions)
    let positions = put(EntityId(3), Position(30, 9), positions)
    let velocities = put(EntityId(3), Velocity(1, 2), empty())
    let velocities = put(EntityId(1), Velocity(2, DecreaseOne), velocities)
    let velocities = put(EntityId(4), Velocity(9, 9), velocities)
    World(positions, velocities)
}

pub move_direct : World -&gt; World
move_direct World(positions, velocities) = {
    let moved = move_positions(join_stores(positions, velocities), positions)
    World(moved, velocities)
}

pub impl StoreAccess&lt;World, Position&gt; {
    component_store World(positions, velocities) = positions
    replace_store (World(positions, velocities), replacement) = World(replacement, velocities)
}

pub impl StoreAccess&lt;World, Velocity&gt; {
    component_store World(positions, velocities) = velocities
    replace_store (World(positions, velocities), replacement) = World(positions, replacement)
}
The module graph remains acyclic: systems depends on stores; world depends on systems and stores. The generic algorithm does not need to import a world that imports it back. The module rules check these dependencies and the public facts crossing each boundary.
Select the world’s facts explicitly at the generic entry:
import stores (Rows, Position, Velocity)
import systems (joined, move_world)
import world (World, initial, impl StoreAccess)

main : Unit -&gt; (Rows&lt;(Position, Velocity)&gt;, World, World)
main () = {
    let original = initial ()
    (joined original, move_world original, original)
}
ash querying-an-immutable-world/src/02-generic-movement.ash
This produces the same complete joined rows, moved world, and original world as the direct entry, with the same tuple type. Each is checked against the independently specified component values above; equality between two faulty implementations would not establish the intended movement.
Selected evidence lowers to ordinary functions and applications. There is no runtime search through the world’s fields. The two methods provide access; their types do not promise that an implementation preserves unrelated fields or that a join matches identifiers. Those properties come from the implementations and calculations we have just followed.

A well-typed join can still match the wrong entities
A positional zip looks plausible because the two lists have compatible lengths and every selected value has the expected component type. It pairs the first position with the first velocity, then recurses on the two tails. On our input, that means entity pairs 1/3, 2/1, and 3/4.
Keep both identifiers in each zipped row so the mismatch is visible. Here the outer Row identifier comes from positions and the identifier inside its payload comes from velocities. move_zipped deliberately ignores the latter identifier when calculating new positions:
import stores (EntityId, Position, Velocity, Store, Rows, NoRows, Row, entries, put)
import systems (advance, joined)
import world (World, initial, impl StoreAccess)

zip_rows : (Rows&lt;Position&gt;, Rows&lt;Velocity&gt;) -&gt; Rows&lt;(EntityId, Position, Velocity)&gt;
zip_rows (NoRows, velocities) = NoRows
zip_rows (Row(entity, position, rest), NoRows) = NoRows
zip_rows (Row(entity, position, rest), Row(other, velocity, later)) = {
    Row(entity, (other, position, velocity), zip_rows(rest, later))
}

move_zipped : (Rows&lt;(EntityId, Position, Velocity)&gt;, Store&lt;Position&gt;) -&gt; Store&lt;Position&gt;
move_zipped (NoRows, positions) = positions
move_zipped (Row(entity, (other, position, velocity), rest), positions) = {
    let updated = put(entity, advance(position, velocity), positions)
    move_zipped(rest, updated)
}

main : Unit -&gt; (Rows&lt;(EntityId, Position, Velocity)&gt;, Rows&lt;Position&gt;, Rows&lt;(Position, Velocity)&gt;)
main () = {
    let original = initial ()
    let World(positions, velocities) = original
    let wrong = zip_rows(entries positions, entries velocities)
    (wrong, entries(move_zipped(wrong, positions)), joined original)
}
